WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
The faculty must schedule six

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.770
hundred exams into three weeks with

3
00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.090
no student sitting two at once.

4
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.740
An exact solver runs all

5
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.100
weekend and does not finish.

6
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.320
A simple heuristic finds a

7
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.740
clash-free timetable in a minute.

8
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.480
Is it the best one?

9
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.860
Today: why some problems are too large for

10
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.560
exact methods, how local search gets

11
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.840
stuck, and how simulated annealing escapes

12
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.340
by sometimes accepting worse moves.

13
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.100
Many scheduling and routing problems are

14
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.360
NP-hard, so exact methods may take

15
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.600
impossibly long on large instances.

16
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.700
Heuristics trade the guarantee of

17
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.180
optimality for good solutions found

18
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:49.540
quickly.

19
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.460
They need careful evaluation instead.

20
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.110
Local search starts from a solution

21
00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.330
and repeatedly moves to a better

22
00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.490
neighbour, for example by moving

23
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.070
one exam to another slot.

24
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.390
It stops when no neighbour is better.

25
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.260
That point is a local optimum,

26
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.580
which may be far from the best.

27
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.080
Simulated annealing sometimes

28
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.400
accepts a worse move, with

29
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.620
probability e to the minus delta over T.

30
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.780
At high temperature it explores freely.

31
00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:26.950
As T cools, it settles, and with slow

32
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.730
enough cooling it can escape local optima.

33
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.720
A move that is worse by two is accepted

34
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.520
with probability e to the minus two at

35
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.980
temperature one, about fourteen percent.

36
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.450
At temperature ten it is accepted about

37
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.870
eighty two percent of the time.

38
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.760
Cooling turns exploration into refinement.

39
00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.470
Students often report one run of a

40
00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.150
heuristic and call it the answer.

41
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.960
Require several runs with different

42
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.980
seeds, a comparison with a lower bound or

43
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.440
an exact solution on small instances, and

44
00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.680
a plot of cost against time.

45
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.340
Quick check!

46
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.750
Local search stops with eight clashes

47
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.630
left, and no single exam move reduces

48
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.050
them.

49
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.860
What has it found?

50
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.260
A local optimum.

51
00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.970
No neighbour is better, but a

52
00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.010
sequence of moves, some worse at

53
00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.650
first, might reach zero clashes.

54
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.980
In the app, open the annealing lab.

55
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.140
Run local search and annealing on the

56
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.640
same problem, change the cooling rate,

57
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.560
and compare the final costs.

58
00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.330
Your turn.

59
00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.700
At temperature two, how likely is

60
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.880
annealing to accept a move that is worse

61
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.500
by two?

62
00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.240
About thirty seven percent:

63
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.200
e to the minus one.

64
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.320
Remember: local search finds local

65
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.340
optima, annealing escapes by accepting

66
00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.160
some worse moves, and heuristics must

67
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.200
be evaluated over many runs.

68
00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.970
Try it in your next lecture: give each

69
00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.350
row of students a different random seed

70
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.370
and the same timetable problem.

71
00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.160
Each row runs annealing and

72
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.360
reports its final cost.

73
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.620
Plot all the results on the

74
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.820
board and discuss the spread.
